# Valgrind/Memcheck suppressions for leak-checking colstore.
#
# Two kinds of entry, with different safety arguments:
#
#  (1) Runtime/dependency machinery that legitimately holds memory for the life
#      of the process (OpenMP thread pool, NumPy SIMD caches, the dynamic
#      loader). These suppress only `reachable`/`possible` -- never `definite`
#      or `indirect` -- so a real colstore leak is never hidden.
#
#  (2) CPython import & interpreter startup (the section at the bottom). On a
#      Python NOT built --with-valgrind (conda, most system Pythons), the
#      importer deserialises .pyc code objects and the interpreter interns
#      strings at startup that are never freed before exit. The OS reclaims
#      them, so they are not real leaks, but Memcheck reports them as
#      definitely/indirectly lost in their thousands. These stanzas DO suppress
#      `definite`/`indirect`, which is safe ONLY because each is anchored on a
#      frame that can never sit above colstore's own code (see that section).
#
# PYTHONMALLOC=malloc (set by run_valgrind.sh) avoids the bulk of pymalloc-arena
# false positives up front; it does not help with (2), which are genuine
# malloc'd objects the interpreter simply never frees.

# ---- OpenMP runtime (libgomp / Intel libomp) -------------------------------
# The thread pool, per-thread stacks, and TLS are allocated on the first
# parallel region and live until exit -- reachable, not leaked.
{
   colstore-openmp-libgomp-reachable
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable,possible
   ...
   obj:*/libgomp.so.*
}
{
   colstore-openmp-libomp-reachable
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable,possible
   ...
   obj:*/libomp.so*
}
{
   colstore-openmp-gomp-thread-start
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable,possible
   fun:malloc
   ...
   fun:gomp_*
}
{
   colstore-openmp-kmp-thread-start
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable,possible
   fun:*alloc
   ...
   fun:__kmp_*
}

# ---- NumPy (CPU-feature detection, SIMD dispatch caches) -------------------
{
   colstore-numpy-cpu-feature-cache
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable,possible
   ...
   obj:*/numpy/*.so
}
{
   colstore-numpy-multiarray-init
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable,possible
   ...
   fun:PyInit__multiarray_umath
}

# ---- Dynamic loader / TLS (dlopen of the extensions) -----------------------
{
   colstore-ld-dlopen-reachable
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable,possible
   ...
   fun:_dl_open
}
{
   colstore-ld-tls-allocate
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable,possible
   ...
   fun:_dl_allocate_tls
}
{
   colstore-ld-init-reachable
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable,possible
   ...
   obj:*/ld-*.so
}

# ---- CPython interpreter state held for the process lifetime ---------------
# Belt-and-suspenders for the small amount that survives PYTHONMALLOC=malloc
# (interned strings, type objects, the small-int cache).
{
   colstore-cpython-pymain-init
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable,possible
   ...
   fun:_PyRuntime*
}
{
   colstore-cpython-interned-reachable
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   ...
   fun:PyUnicode_InternInPlace
}

# ---- CPython import & interpreter startup (definite/indirect, one-time) -----
# See note (2) at the top. Each stanza is anchored ONLY on a frame that runs
# before, and independently of, any gather/reader/writer call, so a real
# colstore leak can never match it:
#
#   * r_object / marshal_loads -- the marshal reader; only ever deserialising a
#     .pyc. colstore C code is never called from it. This covers the dominant
#     family (the bulk of the import-time records).
#   * init_interp_main / Py_InitializeFromConfig -- interpreter core init, which
#     completes before the workload imports colstore.
#
# Broader import frames (PyModule_ExecDef, _imp_exec_builtin, _imp_create_dynamic,
# PyImport_ImportModuleLevelObject) are deliberately NOT used: colstore's own
# extension init runs under them, so anchoring there could mask a genuine
# import-time leak. Residual stdlib/NumPy C-module-init noise these do not catch
# is left for run_valgrind.sh, which judges only leaks attributable to colstore.
{
   cpython-marshal-read-object
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect,possible
   ...
   fun:r_object
}
{
   cpython-marshal-loads
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect,possible
   ...
   fun:marshal_loads
}
{
   cpython-interp-core-init
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect,possible
   ...
   fun:init_interp_main
}
{
   cpython-initialize-from-config
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect,possible
   ...
   fun:Py_InitializeFromConfig
}
